633	//! The regions a layer's picture carries — [`Spotlight`],
634	//! [`ObscureRegion`], the [`SpotShape`] both are drawn with and the
635	//! [`MAX_OBSCURE`] budget on the second (spec §4.2, §5.2).
636	//!
637	//! Split out of `model_props.rs` under the size rule (core/CLAUDE.md: a
638	//! module over ~600 lines gives something up before it takes anything on)
639	//! when road map 2's phase 3c added `video.obscure` and pushed it over.
640	//! `model.rs` → `model_query.rs` in phase 0, and `model.rs` →
641	//! `model_props.rs` in phase 2, are the precedent, and so is their rule:
642	//! the cut goes along a seam somebody can NAME, and the piece taken out is
643	//! re-exported whole so not one path anywhere else changes.
644	//!
645	//! # The seam: one frame, one shape
646	//!
647	//! These are not three property types that happened to be adjacent. A
648	//! region is a box drawn ON the picture, and every box here is in the same
649	//! frame:
650	//!
651	//! > `x` and `y` are the region's CENTRE, as an offset in SOURCE pixels
652	//! > from the centre of the layer's CROPPED rect, y down; `width` and
653	//! > `height` are its size in those same pixels.
654	//!
655	//! Nothing here is in canvas pixels, and nothing here is a fraction of
656	//! anything. That is deliberate and it is the whole reason the frame is
657	//! worth a module of its own: a box in source pixels rides with the layer
658	//! it was drawn on, through `scale`, `x`/`y`, `rotation` and a nest's
659	//! transform, so the region stays on the face — or the password field, or
660	//! the notification — it was put on. A layer's own crop is the only edit
661	//! that moves it, which is why the origin is the CROPPED rect's centre and
662	//! not the source's: crop trims in place (see [`crate::model::Crop`]), so
663	//! measuring from the trimmed centre is what keeps a centred region
664	//! centred. `edit_zoom.rs` calls this frame Q and writes out the equation
665	//! that turns a length in it into canvas pixels; that module doc is the
666	//! canonical derivation and nothing here repeats it.
667	//!
668	//! The second half of the seam is [`SpotShape`]. A region needs a distance
669	//! field to feather against, both of these need the same two — a rectangle
670	//! and an ellipse — and shared vocabulary has exactly one home
671	//! (core/CLAUDE.md). Two enums naming the same two shapes would be two
672	//! things to keep in step in Rust, in `ProjectSnapshot.swift`, in the
673	//! agent's enum check and in `Compositor.metal`.
674	//!
675	//! What is NOT here: the fields that HOLD these values —
676	//! `VideoProps.spotlight` and `VideoProps.obscure` — which are part of the
677	//! video group's own shape and stay in `model_props.rs` with it; and every
678	//! effect that acts on the whole layer rather than a box inside it
679	//! (filters, chroma key, colour, reflection, shadow), which have no frame
680	//! to share.
681	//!
682	//! # Paths
683	//!
684	//! `model_props.rs` re-exports this module whole with
685	//! `pub use crate::model_regions::*`, and `model.rs` re-exports
686	//! `model_props.rs` the same way, so `crate::model_props::Spotlight`,
687	//! `crate::model::Spotlight` and the crate-root re-exports in `lib.rs` all
688	//! still resolve and no other file needed an import changed. The schema
689	//! rules are `model.rs`'s too: the field names ARE the JSON keys,
690	//! declaration order IS key order, every type is `#[serde(default)]` over a
691	//! hand-written [`Default`] that says what an absent key means, and nothing
692	//! here carries `deny_unknown_fields`.
